WEBVTT - Most Fascinating Stories - Episode 35: Esther Hobart Morris and the Equality State

1
00:00:03.040 --> 00:00:05.520
[Rebecca] Welcome to Most Fascinating Stories.

2
00:00:05.520 --> 00:00:06.800
[Rebecca] I'm Rebecca.

3
00:00:06.800 --> 00:00:16.320
[Rebecca] Fifty years before the 19th Amendment granted American women the right to vote, the untamed, male-dominated frontier territory of Wyoming shocked the world.

4
00:00:16.320 --> 00:00:29.760
[Rebecca] In 1869, amid the sagebrush plains and rowdy gold camps of the Rocky Mountains, Wyoming lawmakers passed a revolutionary bill granting women full equal suffrage and the right to hold public office.

5
00:00:29.760 --> 00:00:45.360
[Rebecca] Months later, in the muddy, rough and tumble mining camp of South Pass City, a tall, imposing 55-year-old pioneer woman named Esther Hobert Morris was appointed Justice of the Peace, making her the first female judge in modern history.

6
00:00:45.360 --> 00:00:50.080
[Rebecca] Here is George with the story.

7
00:01:00.960 --> 00:01:10.880
[George] Discovered along the historic Oregon Trail, the Carissa Gold Load had drawn thousands of prospectors, saloons, gambling halls, and dancehouses.

8
00:01:10.880 --> 00:01:14.720
[George] Life in South Pass was rough, muddy, and dangerous.

9
00:01:14.720 --> 00:01:25.520
[George] Men outnumbered women by at least six to one, and legal disputes over mining claims, drunken brawls, and unpaid debts were settled more often with six shooters than with law books.

10
00:01:25.520 --> 00:01:30.400
[George] Yet into this wild frontier setting stepped a group of forward-thinking pioneers.

11
00:01:30.400 --> 00:01:40.160
[George] In 1869, when the newly organized Wyoming Territorial Legislature convened in Cheyenne, South passed saloon keeper and lawmaker William H.

12
00:01:40.480 --> 00:01:49.440
[George] Bright introduced a groundbreaking piece of legislation, an act to grant to the women of Wyoming Territory the right of suffrage and to hold office.

13
00:01:49.440 --> 00:02:01.720
[George] While some lawmakers supported it for genuine democratic principles, others believed it would attract more women settlers to the frontier, and still others thought it was a political joke that the governor would veto.

14
00:02:01.720 --> 00:02:06.760
[George] But on December 10, 1869, Republican Governor John A.

15
00:02:07.000 --> 00:02:15.160
[George] Campbell signed the bill into law, making Wyoming the first place in the world to guarantee women the right to vote on equal terms with men.

16
00:02:15.160 --> 00:02:26.280
[George] Wind howled through the granite passes of the Continental Divide, carrying dust across the unpaved streets where prospectors panned for gold and brawled over disputed placer boundaries.

17
00:02:26.280 --> 00:02:35.400
[George] The legal system was practically non-existent, leaving disputes to be resolved by fistfights or impromptu miners' meetings held in canvas tents.

18
00:02:35.400 --> 00:02:47.240
[George] Stagecoaches arrived twice weekly, kicking up clouds of alkaline dust as prospectors crowded the boardwalks, discussing rumors of rich quartz veins discovered in the surrounding crags.

19
00:02:47.240 --> 00:02:53.080
[George] Behind this political milestone stood an extraordinary woman, Esther Hobert Morris.

20
00:02:53.080 --> 00:03:06.280
[George] Born in Tioga County, New York, in 1814, Esther had been orphaned at age 11, apprenticed as a seamstress, and run a successful hat business before becoming a widow and moving west to Illinois.

21
00:03:06.280 --> 00:03:15.240
[George] There, she witnessed firsthand the legal disenfranchisement of women when she was denied the legal right to inherit her deceased first husband's property.

22
00:03:15.240 --> 00:03:28.040
[George] Standing nearly six feet tall, with piercing hazel eyes and a formidable, no-nonsense presence, Esther married merchant John Morris and followed the gold rush to South Pass City in 1869.

23
00:03:28.040 --> 00:03:44.920
[George] Local folklore recounts that before the territorial election, Esther hosted an intimate tea party in her crude log cabin, inviting candidates William Bright and Herman Nickerson, and extracting a solemn promise from both men that whoever was elected would introduce a woman's suffrage bill.

24
00:03:45.200 --> 00:03:55.920
[George] Whether legend or fact, Esther's commanding intellect, quiet moral courage, and persuasive conviction made her the undisputed civic pillar of the South Pass mining community.

25
00:03:55.920 --> 00:04:05.040
[George] Born in New York in 1814, Esther had been orphaned as a young girl, learning independence early as a skilled milliner and businesswoman.

26
00:04:05.040 --> 00:04:15.760
[George] Her tea party in South Pass City brought together opposing candidates who promised that whichever man won the election would introduce a woman's suffrage bill to the Territorial Assembly.

27
00:04:15.760 --> 00:04:26.800
[George] Morris believed that frontier life required practical courage and moral clarity, often remarking to her neighbors that building a new territory demanded the active wisdom of both men and women.

28
00:04:26.800 --> 00:04:31.680
[George] The true test of Wyoming's revolutionary law arrived just two months later.

29
00:04:31.680 --> 00:04:38.000
[George] In February 1870, South Pass City's male Justice of the Peace, James W.

30
00:04:38.320 --> 00:04:46.320
[George] Stillman, resigned in protest when women were permitted to vote and serve on juries, refusing to hand over the court docket book.

31
00:04:46.320 --> 00:04:48.240
[George] Local district judge John H.

32
00:04:48.240 --> 00:04:55.360
[George] King was desperate to find someone respected, fearless, and literate enough to restore order to the chaotic mining camp.

33
00:04:55.360 --> 00:05:01.600
[George] King appointed 55-year-old Esther Hobert Morris as Justice of the Peace for Sweetwater County.

34
00:05:01.600 --> 00:05:05.600
[George] The announcement sent shockwaves of disbelief across the nation.

35
00:05:05.600 --> 00:05:21.840
[George] National newspapers in New York, Chicago, and London published sarcastic, sensational editorials predicting that a female judge would faint at the first sight of blood, burst into tears during cross-examinations, or be chased out of the courtroom by rowdy miners.

36
00:05:21.840 --> 00:05:24.400
[George] But Esther was completely unfazed.

37
00:05:24.400 --> 00:05:36.600
[George] She converted the front room of her modest log cabin into a formal courtroom, set up a wooden table draped with a calico cloth, and swore her judicial oath on February 14, 1870.

38
00:05:36.600 --> 00:05:45.960
[George] Skeptics in neighboring territories mocked the appointment in frontier editorials, predicting that an elderly grandmother would faint at the sight of frontier weapons.

39
00:05:45.960 --> 00:05:54.440
[George] But the women of the territory celebrated, recognizing that history was being made in a humble cabin 8,000 feet up in the Rocky Mountains.

40
00:05:54.440 --> 00:06:07.800
[George] When the appointment was officially certified in Cheyenne, newspapers in London and Paris reported on the event with astonishment, wondering how an untamed wilderness could outpace ancient European capitals in democratic reform.

41
00:06:07.800 --> 00:06:16.200
[George] Judge Esther Morris presided over the frontier court with iron discipline, razor-sharp common sense, and complete impartiality.

42
00:06:16.200 --> 00:06:24.280
[George] The very first case brought before her was filed by her predecessor, James Stillman, who refused to relinquish the official court docket.

43
00:06:24.280 --> 00:06:35.560
[George] Esther calmly ruled that she had no jurisdiction over Stillman's past records, dismissed the case, and simply purchased a brand new blank ledger book, opening court without missing a beat.

44
00:06:35.560 --> 00:06:41.160
[George] Over the next eight and a half months, Judge Morris heard seventy civil and criminal cases.

45
00:06:41.160 --> 00:06:55.240
[George] Adjudicating assault and battery brawls between drunk miners, prosecuting claim jumping disputes, ruling on debt collections, and even arresting her own husband, John Morris, when he became drunk and disorderly in public.

46
00:06:55.240 --> 00:06:58.920
[George] Finding him and warning him that the law applied to him as well.

47
00:06:58.920 --> 00:07:09.320
[George] Rowdy prospectors who walked into her courtroom wearing hats or chewing tobacco were promptly ordered to remove their hats, spit outside, and conduct themselves like gentlemen.

48
00:07:09.320 --> 00:07:17.280
[George] The rough miners, expecting an easy mark, quickly developed immense respect for her fair, measured, and decisive rulings.

49
00:07:17.280 --> 00:07:24.960
[George] When rowdy teamsters and miners crowded into the log cabin, Esther maintained total order with a calm gaze and quiet authority.

50
00:07:24.960 --> 00:07:32.720
[George] She insisted that justice on the frontier should be administered with the same dignity and precision as in the highest courts of the East.

51
00:07:32.720 --> 00:07:39.280
[George] Esther Morris's tenure as Justice of the Peace was a stunning triumph that silenced skeptics across the globe.

52
00:07:39.280 --> 00:07:48.080
[George] Of the seventy cases she adjudicated in South Pass City, twenty-seven were appealed by losing litigants to higher territorial district courts.

53
00:07:48.080 --> 00:08:01.040
[George] In every single instance, the territorial Supreme Court upheld her verdicts, without a single decision reversed on appeal, a record of judicial competence that surpassed almost every male judge in the territory.

54
00:08:01.040 --> 00:08:12.240
[George] Furthermore, in March 1870, Esther served on the first all-female grand jury in Laramie, proving that women could evaluate criminal indictments with wisdom and objectivity.

55
00:08:12.240 --> 00:08:26.000
[George] When the gold mines of South Pass began to play out in late 1871, Esther moved to Laramie and later Cheyenne, where she remained a prominent national leader in the women's suffrage movement, speaking alongside Susan B.

56
00:08:26.240 --> 00:08:30.240
[George] Anthony and Elizabeth Cady Stanton at national conventions.

57
00:08:30.240 --> 00:08:34.960
[George] Her flawless record on appeal astonished legal scholars across the country.

58
00:08:34.960 --> 00:08:46.400
[George] In an era when many male frontier judges had their hasty rulings overturned by higher courts, Esther's judicial temperament proved that common sense and fairness were the bedrock of effective law.

59
00:08:46.400 --> 00:08:57.440
[George] When Wyoming applied for statehood in 1889, conservative members of the United States Congress threatened to deny entry unless Wyoming repealed its woman's suffrage clause.

60
00:08:57.440 --> 00:09:01.640
[George] Wyoming's legislature famously telegraphed back to Washington.

61
00:09:01.640 --> 00:09:06.280
[George] We will remain out of the Union a hundred years rather than come in without the women.

62
00:09:06.280 --> 00:09:16.200
[George] Congress backed down, and in 1890, Wyoming entered the Union as the 44th state, adopting the official motto that adorns its seal to this day.

63
00:09:16.200 --> 00:09:17.560
[George] Equal rights.

64
00:09:17.560 --> 00:09:31.160
[George] Esther Hobert Morris lived to see her adopted state achieve this glory, participating in the statehood celebration parade in Cheyenne, where she was presented with the official 44-star American flag on behalf of the women of Wyoming.

65
00:09:31.160 --> 00:09:44.760
[George] The telegram sent by the Wyoming legislature became a rallying cry for suffragists across America, demonstrating that the Western Territory would not compromise its founding commitment to human equality for political expediency.

66
00:09:44.760 --> 00:09:54.680
[George] Esther Hobert Morris passed away peacefully in Cheyenne in 1902 at the age of 87, leaving a legacy that permanently reshaped democratic governance.

67
00:09:54.680 --> 00:10:08.520
[George] In 1960, the state of Wyoming honored its pioneer judge by placing a magnificent nine-foot bronze statue of Esther Hobert Morris inside the National Statuary Hall of the United States Capitol in Washington, D.C.

68
00:10:08.760 --> 00:10:14.360
[George] A second replica stands proudly outside the Wyoming State Capitol building in Cheyenne.

69
00:10:14.360 --> 00:10:19.720
[George] Depicted carrying her law book and walking resolutely forward against the prairie wind.

70
00:10:19.720 --> 00:10:32.120
[George] She remains an immortal pioneer of American justice and equality, proving that the frontier was not just a proving ground for gunslingers and cattle barons, but the birthplace of genuine human liberty.

71
00:10:32.120 --> 00:10:44.920
[George] Today, visitors to the United States Capitol can see Esther Likeness standing among the greatest figures in American history, an enduring symbol of Western grit, legal brilliance, and the relentless march of human progress.

72
00:10:46.800 --> 00:10:58.000
[Rebecca] Esther Hobert Morris in Wyoming's early stand for equal rights proved that true democracy recognizes neither gender nor privilege, but the dignity and capability of every human mind.

73
00:10:58.000 --> 00:11:00.960
[Rebecca] Thank you for listening to most fascinating stories.

74
00:11:00.960 --> 00:11:02.240
[Rebecca] I'm Rebecca.

75
00:11:02.240 --> 00:11:08.160
[Rebecca] Join us next Tuesday as we explore the fire that burned Seattle and created the city beneath the streets.

76
00:11:08.160 --> 00:11:10.480
[Rebecca] Until then, stay curious.

